---
title: Slow-cooked lamb with white wine and potatoes
servings: 6
yield: 6 to 10 servings
tags: [lamb, braise, make-ahead, weekend]
source: Slow Cooked Lamb with White Wine and Potatoes by Alison Roman, https://www.alisoneroman.com/recipes/slow-cooked-lamb
---

Season the @lamb shoulder{1}(bone-in, 4 to 5 lb) generously with @kosher salt{} and @ground black pepper{}. If there is time, refrigerate it uncovered ~{12-24%hours}.

Heat the oven to 275°F (135°C), fan off.

Put the lamb in a #large Dutch oven{} with a lid, at least 6 qt. A roasting pan sealed tightly with foil also works.

Arrange the @waxy potatoes{2%lb}(halved if large), @garlic{2%heads}(unpeeled, halved crosswise), @lemon{1}(halved crosswise) and @oregano{1%bunch} around the lamb. Pour over the @dry white wine{750%ml} and add enough @olive oil{} to float a layer on top.

Cover and cook without opening ~{10%hours}, or ~{12%hours} for a big shoulder. An extra hour does no harm.

Let it cool a little, then move the lamb and potatoes to a platter. Spoon over the garlic and the juices from the pot.

Scatter over the @tender herbs{1-2%cup}(parsley, dill, chives or mint), cut a second @lemon{1} for squeezing, and finish with more @olive oil{}. Serve with @Greek yogurt{}(seasoned with salt, pepper and garlic).

> A boneless leg of lamb, 3 to 4 lb, works too and needs ~{8%hours}. Marjoram or thyme can stand in for the oregano, and labneh for the yogurt.
